**Derived on paper: what is wrong with the existing conditioning.** Write the measured complete score as

`V = S + R = 480.113 - 440.079 = 40.034`.

All three quantities must be in the same supplied score units; a common kappa conversion does not affect the following ratios. For independently produced components with relative error bounded by epsilon, the worst-case relative error bound on their sum is

`|error(V)| / |V| <= epsilon * (|S| + |R|) / |V|`

`= epsilon * 920.192 / 40.034 = 22.9853 * epsilon`.

Thus 1% component errors can produce a 9.20192 absolute score error, or 22.985% of the final value. With exact C1 and only a 1% residual error, the absolute error is 4.40079, or 10.993% of the final value. For independent, zero-mean errors with equal relative RMS size, the corresponding illustrative amplification is 16.2684. Independence and those error magnitudes are assumptions for the examples, not measured properties of the heads.

This is predominantly a representation and prediction-error issue, not evidence that binary64 cannot subtract these numbers accurately. Multiplying binary64 unit roundoff by 22.985 gives an order of magnitude around 10^-15. Strict comparisons can still be sensitive near ties, but learned errors and target mismatch are separate, much larger possible problems. Nor is 40.034 necessarily the margin between two configurations: actual ranking margins can be smaller.

Rescaling either target and undoing the scale in the combiner preserves this conditioning. Moving part of S into R also does not demonstrate better conditioning unless the new errors and combination are analyzed. Computing the exact final 40.034 directly removes this particular two-head reconstruction, but produces one total-value prediction, not three distinct sources.

